---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { PHONE, PHONE_TEL } from '../../../lib/site.ts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Should I get a second opinion on my foundation repair estimate?"
  description="When a second opinion on a foundation repair estimate is worth it in Arizona, and what independent measurement actually changes."
>
  <div class="container">
    <h1 class="page-title">Should I get a second opinion on my foundation repair estimate?</h1>
    <p class="lead">Should you get a second opinion? Yes.</p>

    <p>
      A foundation repair estimate is useful information. It is not automatically the final answer. Someone walked the house, pointed at cracks, measured some floors, and handed you a number — often a large one. The natural next step feels like deciding whether to sign. It isn’t.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>When a second opinion makes sense</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>The estimate feels high relative to the visible problems</li>
        <li>The company that measured the floors also sells the repair</li>
        <li>You were told the work needs to happen quickly</li>
        <li>The explanation focused more on what will happen if you don’t repair than on what the measurements actually show</li>
        <li>You’re not clear whether the movement is settlement, heave, or something else</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What an independent second opinion does</h2>
      <p>It does three practical things:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Separates measurement from sales</li>
        <li>Gives you a plain-language read of what the numbers likely mean</li>
        <li>Creates a clear list of questions to take back to any repair contractor</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In Arizona, expansive soils and seasonal moisture changes produce a lot of cracks, sticking doors, and uneven floors that never turn into structural emergencies. Some do. The difference matters.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>You already have a survey?</h2>
      <p>
        Bring it. A useful second opinion can start from data you already paid for and add independent context. You are not required to get one. Many homeowners move forward without it and are fine. Others wish they had paused.
      </p>
      <div class="contact-box">
        <p><strong>Talk it through</strong></p>
        <a class="phone-link" href={`tel:${PHONE_TEL}`}>{PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
